<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Hotak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1697">1697</a>–<a href="/years/1725">1725</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mir Mahmud Hotak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Hotak was an Afghan ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghilzai">Ghilzai</a> Hotak dynasty, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirwais-hotak">Mirwais Hotak</a>. In 1722 he defeated the Safavid army at Gulnabad and besieged and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-husayn">Shah Sultan Husayn</a> to abdicate. This effectively ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak.jpg" alt="Mahmud Hotak" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.samei · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_Hotak_Coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
